We believe there is a need for clarification of the intent behind the creation of such an oversight authority, first and foremost to ensure the powers and competences are well defined and do not overlap with national authorities' prerogatives. More coordination is needed to reduce the fragmentation of enforcement as it applies to on line intermediaries in recent years. We also believe that the country-of-origin principle should be upheld in order to continue fostering the digital single market. Against this backdrop, the creation of a new authority should be carefully approached, in order to avoid adding a layer of regulatory complexity and divergence. Rather, the setup should be compatible with the mandate and work of national authorities and enable their enhanced cooperation. Other elements that should be taken into a ccount are the multiple layers of authorities involved in the process of identifying and notifying illegal content, as well as potential overlap between authorities and between Member States, including situations involving third-countries, in particular jurisdiction issues. Any attempt to grant enforcement powers to oversight authorities should ensure that sanctions are only imposed when there is a systemic failure to comply.

Any attempt to grant enforcement powers to oversight authorities should ensure that sanctions are only imposed when there is a systemic failure to comply. The concept of "systemic failure" should be well defined and sanctioning should take into account several aspects such as:

• Gravity and nature of the infringement: sanctions should take into account the circumstances of the infringement, such as its nature, process and reason, the number of individuals affected and the damage suffered;

• Intention: whether the infringement was intentional or unintentional;

• Mitigation: whether actions have been taken by the hosting provider to mitigate the damage;

• Precautionary measures: measures undertaken by the hosting provider to comply with technical and organizational requirements;

• History: any relevant previous infringements which showcase that the hosting provider has a history of systemic non-compliance;

• Cooperation: actions undertaken by the hosting provider to cooperate with the relevant authorities to identify and remedy the infringement;

