4. Providers who voluntarily go beyond their applicable responsibilities would benefit from a good faith defense from liability under applicable laws for such actions, so long as they acted reasonably; and
5. A regulator with broad enforcement powers would oversee services' compliance with their responsibilities.3 Notably, this framework should not extend to content that infringes copyright or other property/economic rights. Addressing intellectual property infringements requires a different balance of responsibilities between providers, users, and right holders. For this reason, we believe that intellectual property should be regulated independently from digital safety. The details of this framework will benefit from the robust, inclusive consultation process the Commission is undertaking to ensure regulation is technically feasible, practical, and targeted at specific and real harms, all while respecting of the rule of law and champio ning human rights. Microsoft appreciates the enormity of the task at hand and looks forward to continued engagement with all stakeholders as the Digital Services Act consultation proceeds. 3 The regulator should have a mandate to take into account all relevant policy objectives (including, e.g., freedom of expression, freedom of association, privacy, etc.) in making its determinations, not just protecting users from illegal or harmful UGC.

